#!/usr/bin/env bash
# iso/bin/build -- the T5 fork's single build entry point.
#
# docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §1: "bin/build does, in order: verify the
# submodule is exactly at UPSTREAM (refuse otherwise); rsync overlay/ over a
# scratch copy of it; git apply --3way the patches, failing loudly on any
# reject; run the guards in §6; then omarchy-iso-make's work with the three
# §3.10 fixes intact."
#
# The overlay is still EMPTY (T5a) -- no configs/airootfs content, no patches.
# T5a's job was to prove the pipeline reproduces the known-good ISO before
# anything gets added on top (§7: "Do not start T5c before T5a proves
# parity").
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# T5b: the runtime pin is now real, and guard 6.4 enforces the coupling
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# iso/RUNTIME existed before this slice but nothing read it. That is the
# hole docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §0 is about: the git ref and the package
# channel are TWO independent pins, and only one of them is expressible in
# git. `OMARCHY_MIRROR=edge` is a rolling channel with no history -- it
# served omarchy-dev @6d7826d on 2026-08-10 and @1c9dfc5 the next day, and
# the rename in between (`omarchy-setup-system` -> `omarchy-apply-system`)
# means an ISO forked at our UPSTREAM pin but built against today's edge
# calls a binary the runtime no longer ships. It dies at phase 5 of 14, after
# partitioning, LUKS, pacstrap and ~1200 packages.
#
# Two mechanisms close it, and this slice adds both:
#
#   1. --local-source (§2 mechanism A). Upstream's own supported path:
#      builder/build-omarchy-packages.sh builds omarchy-dev,
#      omarchy-settings-dev and omarchy-nvim from mounted git checkouts
#      straight into the offline mirror, and build-iso.sh then EXCLUDES those
#      three from the network `pacman -Syw`. So the runtime the ISO carries
#      comes from iso/RUNTIME's sha rather than from whatever the channel
#      served this morning. It is no longer optional here: a build that falls
#      back to the channel would have an unpinned runtime, which is the
#      defect this slice exists to remove.
#
#      ⚠️ It pins only the three omarchy-* packages. Arch's core/extra/
#      multilib still roll through mirror.omarchy.org (§2). "Reproducible"
#      here means "same inputs by declaration", not bit-identical.
#
#   2. Guard 6.4 (§6.4), in two halves, because neither alone is honest:
#
#      6.4a  BEFORE docker: every /usr/bin/omarchy-* our (patched)
#            orchestrator shells out to exists in the pinned runtime
#            checkout's bin/. Necessary, not sufficient -- the PKGBUILD
#            decides what actually gets installed -- but it costs a grep and
#            it refuses the build before six gigabytes of downloads.
#      6.4b  AFTER docker, on the .pkg.tar.zst files the build actually
#            produced: the same names exist as usr/bin/<name> members of
#            omarchy-dev or omarchy-settings-dev. This is the sufficient
#            half. The two packages are checked as a UNION because that is
#            how they install: omarchy-dev's PKGBUILD ships all of bin/
#            EXCEPT omarchy-debug, omarchy-debug-idle and omarchy-upload-log,
#            which omarchy-settings-dev ships instead, and omarchy-dev
#            hard-depends on omarchy-settings-dev.
#
#      6.4b also asserts the pin TOOK EFFECT: both PKGBUILDs derive pkgver
#      as <base>.r<count>.g<short sha> from the checkout's git history, so a
#      built package whose version does not carry iso/RUNTIME's short sha was
#      not built from the tree we pinned -- whatever else went right.
#
# The name list is DERIVED (grepped out of the scratch tree's orchestrator
# after our patches are applied), never hard-coded, so it cannot go stale
# against our own edits -- §6.4's requirement.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Why this does not just exec upstream's own bin/omarchy-iso-make
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# omarchy-iso-make (iso/upstream/bin/omarchy-iso-make) does two things this
# project's CLAUDE.md forbids doing to the DEV MACHINE, unconditionally, on
# every invocation:
#
#   1. `sudo rm -rf /var/cache/pacman/pkg/*` on the HOST, before Docker even
#      starts. On this dev machine that cache holds 2700+ packages
#      (docs/PROGRESS.md §3.10 item 3 / §7). There is no flag to skip this.
#   2. Bind-mounts the (now-emptied) host cache read-write into a --privileged
#      container.
#
# That is guard 6.3 (docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §6.3): "Do not inherit the
# pacman-cache rm -rf... bin/build points the container at a scratch dir
# under iso/.cache/ instead -- same rebuild speed, no blast radius." The only
# way to get that is to stop calling omarchy-iso-make and drive
# builder/build-iso.sh's Docker invocation ourselves, keeping everything else
# (image, --privileged, the env vars build-iso.sh reads) identical.
#
# Guard 6.1 (ref/mirror agreement) is handled the same way: hard-coded here,
# with the environment refused rather than silently overridden if it disagrees.
#
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Where things land
# --------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# Never in this repo. All scratch state -- the overlaid upstream checkout,
# the pacman package cache, the offline-mirror download cache, and the built
# ISO itself -- lives under $OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR (default
# ~/.cache/omarchy-deck/iso-build), a sibling of the repo, not a subdirectory
# of it. Multi-gigabyte artifacts belong outside version control
# (docs/PROGRESS.md §1.1) and outside this worktree.
#
# Usage:
#   iso/bin/build
#   OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR=/some/scratch/dir iso/bin/build
#
# Env vars:
#   OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR   scratch root (default: see above)
#   OMARCHY_INSTALL_DEBUG        passed through to build-iso.sh unchanged
#   OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC      path to an existing basecamp/omarchy
#                                 checkout. Default: a clone this script
#                                 manages under the scratch root, checked out
#                                 at iso/RUNTIME's sha. When you point this at
#                                 a checkout of your own it is VERIFIED, never
#                                 moved: a HEAD that is not iso/RUNTIME's pin,
#                                 or a dirty tree, is refused. Your checkout is
#                                 an input to the packages this builds
#                                 (makepkg copies the whole tree, untracked
#                                 files included), so "dirty" really is
#                                 "unreproducible", not pedantry.
#   OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC         path to an existing omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs
#                                 checkout -- the PKGBUILDs. REQUIRED unless
#                                 iso/PKGS exists, because this repo does not
#                                 yet pin that third input; see step 2b.
#   OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_NO_NETWORK_HOST
#                                 unset by default. build-iso.sh downloads
#                                 hundreds of packages; Docker's default
#                                 bridge network is throttled to ~2 KB/s on
#                                 this dev machine (docs/PROGRESS.md §7), so
#                                 `docker run --network host` is the default.
#                                 Set this to 1 to fall back to Docker's
#                                 normal bridge network instead.
#
# Exit codes: 0 success. Any failure below is fail-loud (set -euo pipefail
# plus explicit checks) -- CLAUDE.md: "never silently swallow a failure."

set -euo pipefail

BUILD_PROG=iso-build
build_log()  { printf '[%s] %s\n' "$BUILD_PROG" "$*"; }
build_fail() { printf '[%s] ERROR: %s\n' "$BUILD_PROG" "$*" >&2; exit 1; }

ISO_ROOT=$(cd -- "$(dirname -- "${BASH_SOURCE[0]}")/.." && pwd)
REPO_ROOT=$(cd -- "$ISO_ROOT/.." && pwd)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Pin helpers. Three inputs want the same treatment (iso/UPSTREAM, iso/RUNTIME
# and -- if it ever exists -- iso/PKGS), and one implementation of "is this
# checkout exactly at its pin?" is the only way all three stay equally strict.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# parse_pin <file> -- sets PIN_SLUG and PIN_SHA from a one-line 'owner/repo@sha'.
PIN_SLUG=""
PIN_SHA=""
parse_pin() {
  local file=$1 line
  [[ -f $file ]] || build_fail "missing $file"
  line=$(head -n1 "$file")
  [[ $line =~ ^([^/]+/[^@]+)@([0-9a-f]{7,40})$ ]] ||
    build_fail "$file does not look like 'owner/repo@sha': '$line'"
  PIN_SLUG=${BASH_REMATCH[1]}
  PIN_SHA=${BASH_REMATCH[2]}
}

# verify_checkout_at_pin <dir> <sha> <label> <remedy> -- sets CHECKOUT_HEAD.
#
# Sets a global rather than echoing its result: build_fail's `exit 1` inside a
# command substitution would only kill the subshell, and relying on set -e to
# turn that into a real exit is exactly the kind of "fails, but only if you
# read it right" construct this project keeps getting bitten by.
#
# Refuses anything that is not a clean git checkout sitting exactly on <sha>.
# Deliberately NOT "checkout it for us": when the caller supplied the path, a
# disagreement is a disagreement -- guard 6.1's shape (§6.1) -- and silently
# moving someone's working tree onto a different commit is the same class of
# error as silently overriding their OMARCHY_MIRROR. Prints the resolved HEAD
# so a green line is evidence, not reassurance.
CHECKOUT_HEAD=""
verify_checkout_at_pin() {
  local dir=$1 sha=$2 label=$3 remedy=$4 dirty
  CHECKOUT_HEAD=""
  [[ -e "$dir/.git" ]] || build_fail "$label: $dir is not a git checkout. $remedy"
  CHECKOUT_HEAD=$(git -C "$dir" rev-parse HEAD) ||
    build_fail "$label: could not read HEAD of $dir"
  case $CHECKOUT_HEAD in
    "$sha"*) ;;
    *) build_fail "$label: $dir is at $CHECKOUT_HEAD, but the pin is $sha. Refusing to build from an unpinned checkout -- $remedy" ;;
  esac
  dirty=$(git -C "$dir" status --porcelain)
  [[ -z $dirty ]] ||
    build_fail "$label: $dir has local modifications, so it is not 'exactly at the pin'. These files go into the build (makepkg copies the whole tree, untracked included), which makes the result unreproducible:"$'\n'"$dirty"
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1. Verify the submodule is exactly at UPSTREAM. Refuse otherwise.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

parse_pin "$ISO_ROOT/UPSTREAM"
UPSTREAM_SLUG=$PIN_SLUG
UPSTREAM_PIN=$PIN_SHA

UPSTREAM_DIR="$ISO_ROOT/upstream"
[[ -e "$UPSTREAM_DIR/.git" ]] ||
  build_fail "$UPSTREAM_DIR is not a checked-out submodule. Run: git -C '$REPO_ROOT' submodule update --init iso/upstream"

verify_checkout_at_pin "$UPSTREAM_DIR" "$UPSTREAM_PIN" "iso/upstream" \
  "run 'git -C iso/upstream checkout $UPSTREAM_PIN' (or update iso/UPSTREAM if the pin is meant to move) and re-run."
upstream_head=$CHECKOUT_HEAD

build_log "upstream pin OK: $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 1b. Read the OTHER pin -- the one the channel cannot express.
#
# Parsed here (beside UPSTREAM) so a malformed iso/RUNTIME is refused in the
# same breath as a malformed iso/UPSTREAM. The checkouts themselves are
# materialised in step 2b, once the scratch root exists.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

parse_pin "$ISO_ROOT/RUNTIME"
RUNTIME_SLUG=$PIN_SLUG
RUNTIME_PIN=$PIN_SHA
build_log "runtime pin: $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_PIN (enforced below -- §2 mechanism A)"

# The package names build-iso.sh derives from OMARCHY_ISO_REF (its `case`
# maps quattro|edge|dev|local -> the -dev packages). Named ONCE here and
# passed into the container explicitly rather than letting both sides derive
# them, so the host-side guard 6.4b and the container's package build cannot
# disagree about which files to look for. Same lesson as §3's S1: three
# consumers of one list must read one list.
RUNTIME_PACKAGE=omarchy-dev
SETTINGS_PACKAGE=omarchy-settings-dev
NVIM_PACKAGE=omarchy-nvim

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.1 -- ref and mirror must agree, and this build sets them itself.
#
# omarchy-iso-make's own defaults disagree (OMARCHY_ISO_REF=quattro,
# OMARCHY_MIRROR=stable -- docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §6.1), and the known-
# good ISO this slice must reproduce (docs/PROGRESS.md §1.1,
# docs/findings/T9-iso-comparison.md §2) was built with quattro/edge. Refuse
# to run under any other combination rather than silently overriding one a
# caller has already exported -- a caller who set OMARCHY_MIRROR=stable
# expecting it to take effect must be told no, not built a different ISO
# than they asked for.
#
# T5b note: upstream's `omarchy-iso-make --local-source` sets
# OMARCHY_ISO_REF=local. We deliberately keep 'quattro' while mounting the
# same two source trees, because build-iso.sh treats quattro|edge|dev|local
# identically (all four select the -dev packages) and OMARCHY_ISO_REF's only
# other effects are the /root/omarchy_iso_ref stamp and the debug build-info.
# Switching to 'local' would change that stamp, and with it the one file the
# T5a parity comparison against ~/ISOs/ is measured on
# (docs/findings/T9-iso-comparison.md §2), for no build behaviour at all.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

BUILD_ISO_REF=quattro
BUILD_MIRROR=edge

if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_ISO_REF:-} && $OMARCHY_ISO_REF != "$BUILD_ISO_REF" ]]; then
  build_fail "OMARCHY_ISO_REF=$OMARCHY_ISO_REF is set in the environment, but this fork is pinned to ref '$BUILD_ISO_REF' (guard 6.1, T5-fork-plan.md §6.1). Unset it or bring it into agreement."
fi
if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_MIRROR:-} && $OMARCHY_MIRROR != "$BUILD_MIRROR" ]]; then
  build_fail "OMARCHY_MIRROR=$OMARCHY_MIRROR is set in the environment, but this fork is pinned to mirror '$BUILD_MIRROR' (guard 6.1, T5-fork-plan.md §6.1). Unset it or bring it into agreement."
fi

build_log "guard 6.1 OK: OMARCHY_ISO_REF=$BUILD_ISO_REF OMARCHY_MIRROR=$BUILD_MIRROR (agree)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2. Scratch locations -- all outside this repo (docs/PROGRESS.md §1.1).
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

SCRATCH_ROOT=${OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR:-"$HOME/.cache/omarchy-deck/iso-build"}
case $SCRATCH_ROOT in
  "$REPO_ROOT"|"$REPO_ROOT"/*)
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_BUILD_DIR ($SCRATCH_ROOT) is inside this repo. The ISO and its build cache must never land in git-tracked space -- point it elsewhere." ;;
esac

SCRATCH_SRC="$SCRATCH_ROOT/src"
# Guard 6.3: a SCRATCH pacman cache, never the host's /var/cache/pacman/pkg.
# Persisted across runs deliberately (unlike omarchy-iso-make, which deletes
# and re-mounts the host's every time) -- this is the "same rebuild speed"
# half of the guard's promise.
PACMAN_CACHE_DIR="$SCRATCH_ROOT/pacman-cache"
OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR="$SCRATCH_ROOT/offline-mirror-cache"
RELEASE_DIR="$SCRATCH_ROOT/release"

mkdir -p "$SCRATCH_ROOT" "$PACMAN_CACHE_DIR" "$OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR" "$RELEASE_DIR"
build_log "scratch root: $SCRATCH_ROOT (outside the repo)"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 2b. The --local-source inputs: the pinned runtime, and the PKGBUILDs.
#
# This is what turns iso/RUNTIME from a note into a pin. Both trees are
# bind-mounted into the container in step 7, where
# builder/build-omarchy-packages.sh builds omarchy-{dev,settings-dev,nvim}
# from them straight into the offline mirror and build-iso.sh drops those
# three from its network download.
#
# ⚠️ build-iso.sh keys two DIFFERENT decisions off these mounts: the local
# package build needs both /omarchy-source and /omarchy-pkgs, but the
# omarchy-base.packages / setup-form.sh sources switch on /omarchy-source
# ALONE. Mount only the first and you get an ISO whose package lists came
# from the pinned checkout while its runtime came from the channel -- the §0
# split, rebuilt by hand. So this mounts both or neither, and "neither" is
# not offered.
#
# 🔴 The third input nobody pinned. --local-source needs the PKGBUILDs too,
# and omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs decides what actually lands in /usr/bin (its
# omarchy-dev recipe ships all of bin/ except three files). T5-fork-plan.md
# §2 names it as a requirement but pins only two of the three inputs, so
# there is no iso/PKGS to enforce and choosing its value is an operator
# decision, not this script's. Until that file exists the path must be given
# explicitly: an unpinned input is tolerable, an unpinned input nobody
# noticed is not.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# fetch_pinned_checkout <dir> <slug> <sha> <label>
#
# Only ever used for the clone this script MANAGES under the scratch root.
# An operator-supplied checkout is verified and never touched (see
# verify_checkout_at_pin). Re-runnable: an existing clone is reused, and only
# fetched again when it does not already contain the pinned commit.
fetch_pinned_checkout() {
  local dir=$1 slug=$2 sha=$3 label=$4 url="https://github.com/$2.git"
  if [[ ! -e "$dir/.git" ]]; then
    build_log "$label: cloning $slug into $dir (first run; full history -- the PKGBUILDs' pkgver() counts commits)"
    rm -rf "$dir"
    git clone --quiet "$url" "$dir" ||
      build_fail "$label: could not clone $url into $dir"
  fi
  if ! git -C "$dir" cat-file -e "$sha^{commit}" 2>/dev/null; then
    build_log "$label: $sha not present locally; fetching $slug"
    git -C "$dir" fetch --quiet --tags origin ||
      build_fail "$label: could not fetch $slug into $dir"
  fi
  git -C "$dir" cat-file -e "$sha^{commit}" 2>/dev/null ||
    build_fail "$label: $slug has no commit $sha even after fetching. Is the pin in the right repository?"
  git -C "$dir" checkout --quiet --detach "$sha" ||
    build_fail "$label: could not check $dir out at $sha"
}

if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC:-} ]]; then
  RUNTIME_SRC=$OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC
  [[ -d $RUNTIME_SRC ]] ||
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC=$RUNTIME_SRC is not a directory"
  RUNTIME_SRC=$(cd -- "$RUNTIME_SRC" && pwd)
else
  RUNTIME_SRC="$SCRATCH_ROOT/runtime-src"
  fetch_pinned_checkout "$RUNTIME_SRC" "$RUNTIME_SLUG" "$RUNTIME_PIN" "runtime source"
fi

# Verified the same way whether we checked it out or the caller did -- a
# managed clone that verifies itself by construction would prove nothing.
verify_checkout_at_pin "$RUNTIME_SRC" "$RUNTIME_PIN" "runtime source" \
  "point OMARCHY_DECK_RUNTIME_SRC at a checkout of $RUNTIME_SLUG at $RUNTIME_PIN, unset it to let this script manage one, or update iso/RUNTIME if the pin is meant to move."
RUNTIME_HEAD=$CHECKOUT_HEAD
RUNTIME_SHORT=${RUNTIME_HEAD:0:7}
build_log "runtime pin OK: $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD ($RUNTIME_SRC)"

if [[ -f "$ISO_ROOT/PKGS" ]]; then
  parse_pin "$ISO_ROOT/PKGS"
  PKGS_SLUG=$PIN_SLUG
  PKGS_PIN=$PIN_SHA
  if [[ -n ${OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC:-} ]]; then
    PKGS_SRC=$OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC
    [[ -d $PKGS_SRC ]] || build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC is not a directory"
    PKGS_SRC=$(cd -- "$PKGS_SRC" && pwd)
  else
    PKGS_SRC="$SCRATCH_ROOT/pkgs-src"
    fetch_pinned_checkout "$PKGS_SRC" "$PKGS_SLUG" "$PKGS_PIN" "pkgs source"
  fi
  verify_checkout_at_pin "$PKGS_SRC" "$PKGS_PIN" "pkgs source" \
    "point OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC at a checkout of $PKGS_SLUG at $PKGS_PIN, unset it to let this script manage one, or update iso/PKGS if the pin is meant to move."
  build_log "pkgs pin OK: $PKGS_SLUG@$CHECKOUT_HEAD ($PKGS_SRC)"
else
  [[ -n ${OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC:-} ]] ||
    build_fail "no iso/PKGS pin file and no OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC. --local-source needs the omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs PKGBUILDs as well as the runtime (T5-fork-plan.md §2 mechanism A), and this repo pins only two of the three build inputs. Clone https://github.com/omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs and set OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=<path>, or create iso/PKGS containing 'omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs@<sha>' to pin it properly -- which is the operator decision this slice deliberately did not make for you."
  PKGS_SRC=$OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC
  [[ -d $PKGS_SRC ]] || build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC is not a directory"
  PKGS_SRC=$(cd -- "$PKGS_SRC" && pwd)
  [[ -e "$PKGS_SRC/.git" ]] ||
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC is not a git checkout. Without a sha there is nothing to record and nothing to reproduce."
  pkgs_dirty=$(git -C "$PKGS_SRC" status --porcelain)
  [[ -z $pkgs_dirty ]] ||
    build_fail "OMARCHY_DECK_PKGS_SRC=$PKGS_SRC has local modifications. The PKGBUILDs decide what lands in /usr/bin; an edited copy makes the result unreproducible and guard 6.4b meaningless:"$'\n'"$pkgs_dirty"
  pkgs_head=$(git -C "$PKGS_SRC" rev-parse HEAD)
  build_log "⚠️  pkgs source is UNPINNED: using $PKGS_SRC at $pkgs_head"
  build_log "⚠️  pin it by creating iso/PKGS with: omacom-io/omarchy-pkgs@$pkgs_head"
fi

# All three, because build-omarchy-packages.sh builds all three and stops on
# the first one it cannot find -- an hour into a container that has already
# provisioned an entire Arch image.
for pkg_name in "$RUNTIME_PACKAGE" "$SETTINGS_PACKAGE" "$NVIM_PACKAGE"; do
  [[ -d "$PKGS_SRC/pkgbuilds/$pkg_name" ]] ||
    build_fail "pkgs source $PKGS_SRC has no pkgbuilds/$pkg_name. build-omarchy-packages.sh would fail inside the container after the whole image had been provisioned; refusing here instead."
done

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 3. Fresh scratch clone of the pinned submodule.
#
# A fresh local clone every run (rather than reusing/resetting one in place)
# is what makes this idempotent and re-runnable (CLAUDE.md) without needing
# to reason about what a previous run's `git apply` or rsync left behind.
# --local hardlinks objects from iso/upstream's own object store, so this is
# cheap; it is not pushed anywhere and never leaves the scratch root.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

build_log "cloning pinned upstream into scratch ($SCRATCH_SRC)..."
rm -rf "$SCRATCH_SRC"
git clone --quiet --local --no-hardlinks "$UPSTREAM_DIR" "$SCRATCH_SRC"
git -C "$SCRATCH_SRC" checkout --quiet "$upstream_head"

# archiso is itself a submodule of omarchy-iso (configs/profiledef.sh's
# buildmodes need it; bin/omarchy-iso-make's own first step is
# `git submodule update --init --recursive`). Populate it the same way here.
build_log "populating omarchy-iso's own archiso submodule..."
git -C "$SCRATCH_SRC" submodule update --init --recursive --jobs=8 --quiet ||
  build_fail "could not initialize $SCRATCH_SRC's archiso submodule"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 4. rsync overlay/ over the scratch copy.
#
# T5a's overlay is deliberately empty (.gitkeep placeholders only, excluded
# below) -- content is a later slice's job (T5-fork-plan.md §7). This step
# still runs unconditionally so that an empty overlay build and a populated
# one go through the exact same code path; that is what makes the parity
# proof in this slice meaningful for the ones after it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

build_log "applying overlay/configs/ ..."
rsync -a --exclude='.gitkeep' "$ISO_ROOT/overlay/configs/" "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 5. git apply --3way the patches. Fail loudly on any reject.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

shopt -s nullglob
patches=("$ISO_ROOT"/overlay/patches/*.patch)
shopt -u nullglob

if [[ ${#patches[@]} -eq 0 ]]; then
  build_log "no patches in overlay/patches/ (expected for T5a's empty overlay)"
else
  for p in "${patches[@]}"; do
    build_log "applying patch: $(basename -- "$p")"
    git -C "$SCRATCH_SRC" apply --3way "$p" ||
      build_fail "patch $(basename -- "$p") did not apply cleanly (git apply --3way left a reject or conflict). Upstream moved under this patch -- do not silently paper over it; rebase the patch by hand."
  done
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 6. The guards. 6.1 already ran above (it gates which env vars reach Docker
#    at all, so it has to run before this point). 6.3 is structural -- it is
#    the scratch PACMAN_CACHE_DIR set up in step 2 and the absence, anywhere
#    in this script, of `sudo rm -rf` against a host path. Recorded here so
#    a reader looking for "guard 6.3" finds this comment.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

build_log "guard 6.3 OK: pacman cache is scratch ($PACMAN_CACHE_DIR), host cache untouched"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.4a -- the runtime/finalizer coupling, checked before Docker starts.
#
# T5-fork-plan.md §6.4: "assert that every /usr/bin/omarchy-* the orchestrator
# shells out to actually exists in [the runtime]. Derive the list by grepping
# our patched phases_impl.py ... so the guard cannot go stale against our own
# edits."
#
# Scope is the whole orchestrator directory, not phases_impl.py alone: §3's S3
# puts our configure_deck phase in a NEW module beside it, and a guard that
# only ever reads the file that exists today would go blind to exactly the
# code we are about to add.
#
# Scope stops there on purpose, though. configs/airootfs/usr/local/bin/
# omarchy-install-dashboard also names /usr/bin/omarchy-upload-log, but it
# probes four candidate paths and carries on without any of them -- treating
# a deliberately optional lookup as a hard requirement is the false positive
# that teaches people to ignore a check (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17). The
# orchestrator's calls are subprocess.run(..., check=True) into arch-chroot;
# those are the ones that abort an install two-thirds of the way through.
#
# What this half CANNOT prove: that the package built from this checkout ships
# the file. omarchy-dev's PKGBUILD excludes three names from bin/ (they come
# from omarchy-settings-dev instead), so bin/ is an over-approximation of
# /usr/bin. Guard 6.4b closes that against the real artifact after the build.
# Both are needed; this one is here because it costs a grep and saves ~6 GB.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

ORCHESTRATOR_DIR="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/airootfs/usr/share/omarchy-iso/orchestrator"
[[ -d $ORCHESTRATOR_DIR ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: no orchestrator at ${ORCHESTRATOR_DIR#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} in the overlaid tree. Upstream moved it, or the overlay clobbered it. Refusing to pass a guard that cannot see its subject."

# Trailing '.' stripped: a name at the end of a sentence in a comment or log
# string would otherwise be reported as a missing binary called 'omarchy-foo.'.
mapfile -t REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS < <(
  grep -rhoE '/usr/bin/omarchy-[A-Za-z0-9._-]+' "$ORCHESTRATOR_DIR" |
    sed -E 's|^/usr/bin/||; s/\.+$//' | sort -u
)
(( ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a found no /usr/bin/omarchy-* references in the orchestrator at all. The orchestrator does call the runtime (run_system_finalizer, run_chroot_finalizer), so zero matches means the pattern went stale, not that the coupling went away. A guard with an empty subject is not a passing guard."

[[ -d "$RUNTIME_SRC/bin" ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: the runtime checkout $RUNTIME_SRC has no bin/ directory."
mapfile -t RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES < <(
  find "$RUNTIME_SRC/bin" -maxdepth 1 \( -type f -o -type l \) -name 'omarchy-*' -printf '%f\n' | sort -u
)
(( ${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: $RUNTIME_SRC/bin contains no omarchy-* files. That is not a runtime checkout."

# --- the SECOND provider set: binaries our OWN package ships ---------------
#
# 6.4a's original premise was "every /usr/bin/omarchy-* the orchestrator calls
# comes from the pinned basecamp/omarchy checkout". That premise broke the
# moment our own configure_deck phase started shelling out to
# /usr/bin/omarchy-deck-apply-patches (T12): that binary comes from the
# omarchy-deck package in THIS repo, and the pinned runtime will never ship it.
# Against one provider the guard fails with a completely wrong diagnosis --
# "the pins have drifted" sends the reader off to compare two commits that are
# both fine.
#
# It was dodged rather than fixed for a day: deck_patches.py composes the path
# from APPLIER_DIR + APPLIER_NAME so the grep above cannot see it, kept honest
# by test/unit/test-deck-configure-patches.py running this guard's own regex
# over that file. That works and it is fragile -- the next person writes the
# literal and gets the wrong diagnosis. This is the fix; the dodge can go.
#
# DERIVED, never hand-kept, for exactly the reason the name list above is
# derived (§6.4): an exemption list maintained by hand goes stale against our
# own next edit, and it goes stale silently -- which is worse than not having
# one, because it also buys false confidence. The authority for "what lands in
# /usr/bin" is the package() body of our PKGBUILD, so that is what is read --
# the same question 6.4b asks of the real .pkg.tar.zst, asked here of the
# recipe, before Docker.
#
# Read from the OVERLAID tree, not from iso/overlay/, for the same reason the
# orchestrator is: this is the copy build-iso.sh will be handed.
DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR="$SCRATCH_SRC/configs/deck/pkgbuilds/omarchy-deck"
DECK_PKGBUILD="$DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR/PKGBUILD"
DECK_PROVIDED_BINS=()
DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE=""

if [[ -f $DECK_PKGBUILD ]]; then
  mapfile -t DECK_PROVIDED_BINS < <(
    grep -oE '[$][{]?pkgdir[}]?/usr/bin/[A-Za-z0-9._-]+' "$DECK_PKGBUILD" |
      sed -E 's|.*/usr/bin/||' | sort -u
  )
  if (( ${#DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} > 0 )); then
    DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE="omarchy-deck's PKGBUILD installs ${#DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[@]} into /usr/bin: ${DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[*]}"
  elif grep -qE '[$][{]?pkgdir[}]?/usr/bin' "$DECK_PKGBUILD"; then
    # It writes into /usr/bin and this derivation could not name one thing it
    # puts there -- `install -t "$pkgdir/usr/bin"`, or an `install -d` plus a
    # cp, both of which name their targets somewhere this expression cannot
    # see. Under-deriving is the one outcome that must never be quiet: it
    # would turn into "our package does not ship this either" against a
    # package that ships it perfectly well.
    build_fail "guard 6.4a cannot run: $DECK_PKGBUILD installs into \$pkgdir/usr/bin, but this guard could not derive a single binary NAME from it.

  The derivation looks for an explicit '\$pkgdir/usr/bin/<name>' target, which
  is the form docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §5 specifies and the form
  test/t12-patch-seam-container-e2e.sh proved. A target-directory install
  ('install -t \"\$pkgdir/usr/bin\" ...') names its files somewhere this
  expression cannot see them.

  Name the targets explicitly in package(), or teach this derivation the other
  form -- but do not let it silently resolve to the empty set, because then
  every binary our own package ships would be reported as shipped by nobody."
  else
    DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE="omarchy-deck's PKGBUILD installs nothing into /usr/bin"
  fi
else
  DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE="there is no omarchy-deck PKGBUILD at ${DECK_PKGBUILD#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} in the overlaid tree, so our package provides nothing"
fi

# The union of the two provider sets. It is a union, not a bypass: a name that
# neither ships is still a hard failure, and the message below has to say WHICH
# provider was supposed to have it, because they are different bugs with
# different fixes.
missing_bins=()
deck_supplied_bins=()
for want in "${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]}"; do
  found=0
  for have in "${RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]}"; do
    [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; break; }
  done
  if (( ! found )); then
    for have in "${DECK_PROVIDED_BINS[@]}"; do
      [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; deck_supplied_bins+=("$want"); break; }
    done
  fi
  (( found )) || missing_bins+=("$want")
done

if (( ${#missing_bins[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_fail "guard 6.4a: the orchestrator calls binaries that NO provider ships.

  installer     $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head (iso/UPSTREAM)
  runtime       $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD (iso/RUNTIME, $RUNTIME_SRC)
  our package   $DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE

  Shelled out to, shipped by neither:
$(printf '    /usr/bin/%s\n' "${missing_bins[@]}")
  Two different bugs wear this shape, and the fix is not the same one:

    * THE PINNED RUNTIME DOES NOT SHIP IT. That is T5-fork-plan.md §0: the git
      ref and the package channel are two independent pins and they have
      drifted apart -- a rename or a removal between them, not an empty
      checkout (the runtime offers ${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} omarchy-* binaries in bin/). Move both
      pins together, or neither.

    * OUR PACKAGE DOES NOT SHIP IT EITHER. If the name is one of ours, nothing
      is wrong with the pins at all: the omarchy-deck PKGBUILD's package() body
      does not install it, so add it there. Its current /usr/bin list is on the
      'our package' line above.

  Left alone, either one fails at 'Configuring system', phase 5 of 14, AFTER
  partitioning, LUKS, pacstrap and ~1200 packages."
fi

if (( ${#deck_supplied_bins[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_log "guard 6.4a OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries (${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[*]}) are shipped by a provider -- ${#deck_supplied_bins[@]} by our own omarchy-deck package (${deck_supplied_bins[*]}), the rest by $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT's bin/ (${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} candidates)"
else
  build_log "guard 6.4a OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries (${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[*]}) exist in $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT's bin/ (${#RUNTIME_BIN_NAMES[@]} candidates); $DECK_PROVIDER_NOTE"
fi

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.6 -- every runtime patch still applies to the pinned runtime.
#
# docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §1 point 4: "`git apply --check` every
# patch in src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches/ against the pinned
# basecamp/omarchy checkout, fail on a reject, name the patch and both SHAs."
#
# WHY THIS IS A BUILD GUARD AND NOT A TEST. These patches are not applied here.
# They are applied on the TARGET, at pacman time, by
# omarchy-deck-apply-patches, weeks after this ISO was written. So a patch that
# upstream has moved out from under does not break anything visible: the applier
# reports a reject into /var/lib/omarchy-deck/patch-state.json, and unless
# somebody reads it the Deck simply, quietly, loses the customisation -- the
# lock screen goes back to blanking the panel in ~2 s instead of ~20 s
# (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.24). docs/findings/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §4 is the
# whole argument for this guard in one line: FAIL AT BUILD, NOT AT INSTALL.
# This is the cheapest possible place to discover "the RUNTIME pin moved and
# our patch is stale" -- before an ISO exists at all.
#
# 🔴 --check AND NOTHING ELSE. Not --3way, not fuzz, not a sed fallback. The
# reject IS the product of this seam: the patches carry context lines
# deliberately (the lock timer's literal was retuned three times in two months,
# and a sed would have matched nothing, exited 0, and lost the fix in silence
# -- 0010's .meta says so). Anything that makes a stale patch "apply anyway" is
# the failure mode this mechanism exists to make loud.
#
# Nor --reverse --check as an "already applied, fine" escape hatch:
# verify_checkout_at_pin above refuses a dirty runtime checkout, so "already
# applied" is impossible here by construction, and treating it as a pass would
# mean silently accepting a mutated checkout.
#
# Placed here -- after 6.4a, the last consumer of $RUNTIME_SRC, and before
# Docker -- for 6.4a's reason: it costs a git apply --check per patch and it
# refuses the build before six gigabytes of downloads.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

# WHERE THE PATCHES LIVE. Searched, not hard-coded. They ship INSIDE the
# omarchy-deck package (docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §5: "the patches
# ship in the package, so the package payload is one directory"), and that
# directory was still moving out of src/ and into the package's pkgbuild dir
# while this was being written.
#
# Candidates in preference order. The package's own pkgbuild directory comes
# first, and the directory ITSELF before any subdirectory of it, because
# makepkg's source=() cannot reach outside that directory -- so the payload is
# flat beside the PKGBUILD, which is exactly where a `find -name patches` would
# NOT have looked. Subdirectories are swept too, in case it is ever nested. The
# repo-side src/ home is the last resort, and when both hold patches the run
# says so rather than picking one quietly.
runtime_patch_dirs=()
if [[ -d $DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR ]]; then
  runtime_patch_dirs+=("$DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR")
  while IFS= read -r dir; do
    runtime_patch_dirs+=("$dir")
  done < <(find "$DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR" -mindepth 1 -type d | sort)
fi
if [[ -d "$REPO_ROOT/src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches" ]]; then
  runtime_patch_dirs+=("$REPO_ROOT/src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches")
fi

# A guard with no subject is not a passing guard -- 6.4a's empty-match case,
# inherited. "No patches anywhere" does not mean the seam went away; it means
# the payload went missing, and a silent pass would ship a Deck that quietly
# lost every customisation the seam exists to keep.
(( ${#runtime_patch_dirs[@]} > 0 )) ||
  build_fail "guard 6.6 cannot run: no runtime-patch directory found.

  looked in
    ${DECK_PKGBUILD_DIR#"$SCRATCH_SRC"/} and below   (inside the omarchy-deck package, as shipped)
    src/omarchy-deck-patches/patches                 (the repo-side home)

  These patches are the T12 upstream-patch seam (docs/tasks/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md).
  Their absence is not 'nothing to check' -- it is the payload having gone
  missing, which on the Deck looks like the lock screen blanking the panel in
  ~2 s again with nothing anywhere saying why. If the seam was retired on
  purpose, remove this guard on purpose."

RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR=""
runtime_patches=()
runtime_patch_dupes=()
for dir in "${runtime_patch_dirs[@]}"; do
  shopt -s nullglob
  dir_patches=("$dir"/*.patch)
  shopt -u nullglob
  (( ${#dir_patches[@]} > 0 )) || continue
  if [[ -z $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR ]]; then
    RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR=$dir
    runtime_patches=("${dir_patches[@]}")
  else
    runtime_patch_dupes+=("$dir")
  fi
done

[[ -n $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR ]] ||
  build_fail "guard 6.6 cannot run: a runtime-patch directory exists but holds no *.patch files.

  looked in
$(printf '    %s\n' "${runtime_patch_dirs[@]}")
  An empty patch directory is the same failure as a missing one, and it is the
  likelier of the two: a move that took the directory and left the files, or a
  payload copy step that globbed the wrong thing. Refusing to report a pass
  over an empty set (docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §5.4)."

if (( ${#runtime_patch_dupes[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_log "guard 6.6: checking $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR (the copy the omarchy-deck package ships). Also present: ${runtime_patch_dupes[*]} -- two copies of one patch set is a drift waiting to happen, and only the shipped one is checked here."
fi

# Every failure is collected, not the first one: a RUNTIME pin that moves
# usually breaks more than one patch at once, and rebasing them one build at a
# time is three 40-minute round trips to learn what one run already knew.
patch_rejects=()
for p in "${runtime_patches[@]}"; do
  patch_name=$(basename -- "$p")
  if reject_detail=$(git -C "$RUNTIME_SRC" apply --check -p1 -- "$p" 2>&1); then
    continue
  fi
  # The user-visible consequence comes from the patch's own .meta
  # `requirement:` line -- derived, so it cannot describe a patch this repo no
  # longer carries, and so a new patch gets a real consequence in this message
  # without anybody remembering to add one here.
  consequence=""
  if [[ -f "${p%.patch}.meta" ]]; then
    consequence=$(sed -nE 's/^requirement:[[:space:]]*//p' "${p%.patch}.meta" | head -n1)
  fi
  [[ -n $consequence ]] ||
    consequence="(no 'requirement:' line in ${patch_name%.patch}.meta, so this patch does not say what it is for -- fix that too)"
  patch_rejects+=("    $patch_name
      what it is for: $consequence
      git apply --check said:
$(printf '%s\n' "$reject_detail" | sed 's/^/        /')")
done

if (( ${#patch_rejects[@]} > 0 )); then
  build_fail "guard 6.6: ${#patch_rejects[@]} of ${#runtime_patches[@]} runtime patch(es) no longer apply to the pinned runtime.

  installer   $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head (iso/UPSTREAM)
  runtime     $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD (iso/RUNTIME, $RUNTIME_SRC)
  patches     $RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR

$(printf '%s\n' "${patch_rejects[@]}")
  Upstream moved under these patches. Nothing here is broken yet -- which is
  exactly why this must stop the build: the patches are applied on the TARGET at
  pacman time, so a stale one costs you nothing today and silently drops the
  customisation on every Deck this ISO installs. That is the trade
  docs/findings/T12-upstream-patch-seam.md §4 names: fail at BUILD, not at
  install.

  🔴 Rebase the patch against $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT by hand, and update
  its .meta post-conditions in the same commit. Do NOT reach for --3way, fuzz,
  or a sed fallback to make it 'apply anyway': the reject is the PRODUCT of this
  seam, not an obstacle to it. If upstream made the change deliberately and the
  patch is now pointless, retire the patch and its .meta together."
fi

build_log "guard 6.6 OK: all ${#runtime_patches[@]} runtime patch(es) in ${RUNTIME_PATCH_DIR#"$REPO_ROOT"/} still apply cleanly to $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT ($(printf '%s ' "${runtime_patches[@]##*/}"))"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.5 -- the payload audit (T5-fork-plan.md §6.5, specified in §5.4).
#
# WHY: docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17 and operator decision §5.25 #7. The dev Deck
# keeps /etc/sudoers.d/99-deck-testing on purpose -- the iterate-in-place SSH
# loop needs it, and §5.17 established that no narrower grant does the job --
# and the ISO must REFUSE to carry it. §5.4 is explicit that this FAILS THE
# BUILD. A warning here would be a comment with extra steps: the whole reason
# the file exists on the Deck is that it is convenient, which is exactly the
# pressure that gets a warning ignored.
#
# tools/iso-payload-audit.sh is the check itself (16 assertions of its own in
# test/unit/test-iso-payload-audit.sh). It runs on the HOST, not in the
# container: it sources src/deck-session.sh to share the blanket/passwordless
# predicate with `stage-audit-privileges` rather than reimplement it, so it
# needs this repo's tree, which the container never sees.
#
# Like guard 6.4 this is in two halves, and neither alone is honest.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

PAYLOAD_AUDIT="$REPO_ROOT/tools/iso-payload-audit.sh"

# run_payload_audit <label> <root>... -- sets PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS, and returns
# it so callers can write `if ! run_payload_audit ...; then`.
#
# The status is captured HERE rather than at the call site because `if ! cmd`
# sets $? to the negated value: inside the `then` branch of `if ! cmd`, $? is 0
# no matter what cmd returned. A call site could therefore not tell exit 2
# (usage -- a bug in this wiring) from exit 1 (a real finding in the payload),
# and reporting the second as the first would send the reader hunting through
# the overlay for a file that is not there. Same reason verify_checkout_at_pin
# sets a global: the honest value has to survive the control flow.
PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=0
run_payload_audit() {
  local label=$1
  shift
  PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=0
  # A missing or unrunnable audit is a FAILURE, never a skipped check. In a
  # green build log "the audit found nothing" and "the audit never ran" look
  # identical, and this project has shipped that mistake before (docs/PROGRESS.md
  # §5.30c). 126/127 are the shell's own cannot-execute/not-found codes,
  # borrowed so they cannot collide with the audit's documented 0/1/2.
  if [[ ! -f $PAYLOAD_AUDIT ]]; then PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=127; return 127; fi
  if [[ ! -x $PAYLOAD_AUDIT ]]; then PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=126; return 126; fi
  # Named roots, so a green build says what was audited. The audit prints its
  # own denominator (files/roots/archives inspected) and that is not repeated.
  build_log "payload audit ($label): $*"
  bash "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT" "$@" || PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS=$?
  return "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS"
}

# payload_audit_verdict <status> -- one sentence saying what a status MEANS.
# Shared by both halves so they cannot describe the same code differently.
payload_audit_verdict() {
  case $1 in
    1) printf '%s' "the audit FOUND a sudoers grant giving blanket root with NO password -- its own WARNING lines above name the file and the offending line" ;;
    2) printf '%s' "the audit exited 2, which is its USAGE code. That is a bug in this script's wiring (no roots passed, or a path that is not a directory), not a finding about the payload -- fix the call, do not delete it" ;;
    126) printf '%s' "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT exists but is not executable, so the audit did not run" ;;
    127) printf '%s' "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT does not exist, so the audit did not run" ;;
    *) printf '%s' "the audit exited $1, which is none of its documented codes (0 clean, 1 finding, 2 usage). An unknown status is a failure, not a pass" ;;
  esac
}

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.5a -- before Docker, beside 6.4a, and for the same reason: it costs a
# find(1) and it refuses the build before six gigabytes of downloads.
#
# Two roots, answering different questions:
#
#   $ISO_ROOT/overlay      what this fork ADDS. The likeliest route for the
#                          accident is a file copied out of src/ or off the dev
#                          Deck, where that grant is deliberate.
#   $SCRATCH_SRC/configs   the overlaid, PATCHED airootfs source -- upstream's
#                          tree plus our rsync (step 4) plus our patches
#                          (step 5), i.e. what mkarchiso is about to be handed.
#
# ⚠️ The second root is NOT §5.4's "assembled airootfs work dir", and saying so
# matters more than the guard reading tidily. mkarchiso's real work dir is
# built inside the container and is never bind-mounted out, so nothing on this
# host can read it. $SCRATCH_SRC/configs is the closest thing that exists here:
# it is the exact input to that assembly, so it covers everything WE put in the
# live filesystem -- but not a drop-in that arrives from a package during the
# airootfs pacstrap. That case belongs to 6.5b, which is why there are two.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if ! run_payload_audit "overlay + overlaid airootfs source" \
  "$ISO_ROOT/overlay" "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs"; then
  build_fail "guard 6.5a: $(payload_audit_verdict "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS")

  roots audited
    $ISO_ROOT/overlay        (what this fork adds)
    $SCRATCH_SRC/configs     (upstream + overlay + patches, as handed to mkarchiso)

  docs/tasks/T5-fork-plan.md §5.4: /etc/sudoers.d/99-deck-testing is deliberate
  on the dev Deck (docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17, operator decision §5.25 #7) and an
  ISO that ships one is not a product. Take it out of the payload -- do not
  weaken the audit."
fi

build_log "guard 6.5a OK: no blanket passwordless root in the overlay or in the airootfs source handed to mkarchiso"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 7. The build. Mirrors iso/upstream/bin/omarchy-iso-make's docker invocation
#    of builder/build-iso.sh, minus the two things guard 6.3 forbids (see the
#    file header) and minus the interactive "boot it?" prompt at the end.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

command -v docker >/dev/null 2>&1 || build_fail "docker is required and not on PATH"

NETWORK_ARGS=(--network host)
if [[ ${OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_NO_NETWORK_HOST:-} == 1 ]]; then
  NETWORK_ARGS=()
  build_log "OMARCHY_DECK_ISO_NO_NETWORK_HOST=1: using Docker's default bridge network (slower on this dev machine -- docs/PROGRESS.md §7)"
else
  build_log "using --network host (docs/PROGRESS.md §7: the default bridge is throttled to ~2 KB/s here)"
fi

DOCKER_ARGS=(
  --rm
  --privileged
  "${NETWORK_ARGS[@]}"
  -e "OMARCHY_ISO_REF=$BUILD_ISO_REF"
  -e "OMARCHY_MIRROR=$BUILD_MIRROR"
  -e "OMARCHY_INSTALL_DEBUG=${OMARCHY_INSTALL_DEBUG:-}"
  # Stated rather than re-derived from the ref inside the container, so the
  # names guard 6.4b looks for on the way out are the names that were built.
  -e "OMARCHY_RUNTIME_PACKAGE=$RUNTIME_PACKAGE"
  -e "OMARCHY_SETTINGS_PACKAGE=$SETTINGS_PACKAGE"
  -e "OMARCHY_NVIM_PACKAGE=$NVIM_PACKAGE"
  -e "HOST_UID=$(id -u)"
  -e "HOST_GID=$(id -g)"
  -v "$RELEASE_DIR/:/out/"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/builder/build-iso.sh:/build-iso.sh:ro"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/archiso:/archiso:ro"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/builder:/builder:ro"
  -v "$SCRATCH_SRC/configs:/configs:ro"
  # --local-source (step 2b): both mounts, always together.
  -v "$RUNTIME_SRC:/omarchy-source:ro"
  -v "$PKGS_SRC:/omarchy-pkgs:ro"
  # Guard 6.3: scratch caches, never the host's.
  -v "$PACMAN_CACHE_DIR:/var/cache/pacman/pkg"
  -v "$OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR:/var/cache/airootfs/var/cache/omarchy"
)

build_log "starting docker build (this downloads hundreds of packages; can take a long time on first run)..."
docker run "${DOCKER_ARGS[@]}" archlinux/archlinux:latest /build-iso.sh

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 8. Locate the result. Naming happens further down, AFTER guard 6.4b -- a
#    rejected ISO must not be handed a finished-looking name.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

shopt -s nullglob
built_isos=("$RELEASE_DIR"/*.iso)
shopt -u nullglob
[[ ${#built_isos[@]} -ge 1 ]] ||
  build_fail "docker build reported success but no .iso appeared in $RELEASE_DIR"

# Newest first; mkarchiso writes exactly one per run, but be explicit rather
# than assume. find -printf avoids ls's non-alphanumeric-filename footguns.
latest_iso=$(find "${built_isos[@]}" -maxdepth 0 -printf '%T@ %p\n' | sort -rn | head -n1 | cut -d' ' -f2-)

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.4b -- the same coupling, re-asked of the artifact that will ship.
#
# 6.4a read the runtime CHECKOUT; this reads the .pkg.tar.zst files
# builder/build-omarchy-packages.sh actually produced, which is what the
# target installs. They can differ: omarchy-dev's PKGBUILD ships bin/*
# except omarchy-debug, omarchy-debug-idle and omarchy-upload-log, which
# omarchy-settings-dev ships instead -- so the honest provider set is the
# UNION of the two packages, and only the packages know it.
#
# The packages are readable from here because the container writes the
# offline mirror into a bind mount (build-iso.sh's
# /var/cache/airootfs/var/cache/omarchy). Yes, this runs after the expensive
# part -- 6.4a is the cheap early gate. What this one buys is the difference
# between shipping an ISO that dies at phase 5 and not shipping one at all.
#
# It also answers the question 6.4a cannot: DID THE PIN TAKE EFFECT? Both
# PKGBUILDs compute pkgver as <base>.r<count>.g<short sha> from the source
# tree's git history, so a package version that does not carry iso/RUNTIME's
# short sha means the offline mirror holds a channel build, not ours --
# --local-source silently not having applied is precisely the failure this
# slice exists to prevent, and it is invisible in a green build log.
#
# ⚠️ OWED: the second provider set 6.4a just grew is NOT here yet. 6.4a now
# accepts a name that our own omarchy-deck package's PKGBUILD installs into
# /usr/bin; this half still asks only omarchy-dev + omarchy-settings-dev. So
# the day a literal /usr/bin/omarchy-deck-* appears in the orchestrator, 6.4a
# passes and THIS guard fails -- after Docker, with the wrong diagnosis
# ("check pkgbuilds/omarchy-dev's package() exclusion list"), which is the same
# wrong-diagnosis bug 6.4a was just fixed for, moved six gigabytes later. The
# fix is symmetric: read the built omarchy-deck package out of the offline
# mirror and add its usr/bin members to PROVIDED_BINS. Deliberately left for
# the slice that owns the built package, and it needs its own negative test.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR="$OFFLINE_MIRROR_CACHE_DIR/mirror/offline"

reject_iso() {
  if [[ -f $latest_iso ]]; then
    mv -f "$latest_iso" "$latest_iso.rejected"
    build_log "renamed the built ISO to $(basename -- "$latest_iso").rejected -- it must not be flashed, and a later run's *.iso glob must not pick it up"
  fi
  build_fail "$1"
}

# inspect_package <file> -- sets PKG_NAME, PKG_VER, PKG_BINS.
PKG_NAME=""
PKG_VER=""
PKG_BINS=()
inspect_package() {
  local file=$1 pkginfo listing
  # Captured, not piped: bsdtar failing mid-read inside a process
  # substitution would look exactly like a package with no members.
  pkginfo=$(bsdtar -xOf "$file" .PKGINFO) ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: could not read .PKGINFO from $file"
  listing=$(bsdtar -tf "$file") ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: could not list the members of $file"
  PKG_NAME=$(printf '%s\n' "$pkginfo" | sed -n 's/^pkgname = //p' | head -n1)
  PKG_VER=$(printf '%s\n' "$pkginfo" | sed -n 's/^pkgver = //p' | head -n1)
  [[ -n $PKG_NAME && -n $PKG_VER ]] ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: $file has no pkgname/pkgver in .PKGINFO"
  mapfile -t PKG_BINS < <(printf '%s\n' "$listing" | sed -n 's|^usr/bin/\(..*\)$|\1|p' | sort -u)
}

command -v bsdtar >/dev/null 2>&1 ||
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b needs bsdtar (libarchive) to read the built packages and it is not on PATH. Refusing to skip the check that reads the artifact -- install libarchive and re-run."
[[ -d $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR ]] ||
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b: no offline mirror at $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR after a successful build. The container should have written the locally built packages there."

PROVIDED_BINS=()
for pkg_name in "$RUNTIME_PACKAGE" "$SETTINGS_PACKAGE"; do
  shopt -s nullglob
  pkg_candidates=("$OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR/$pkg_name-"*.pkg.tar.*)
  shopt -u nullglob

  pkg_file=""
  pkg_version=""
  for candidate in "${pkg_candidates[@]}"; do
    [[ $candidate == *.sig ]] && continue
    inspect_package "$candidate"
    # The glob is a prefix match; .PKGINFO is the authority on which package
    # this file actually is.
    [[ $PKG_NAME == "$pkg_name" ]] || continue
    [[ -z $pkg_file ]] ||
      reject_iso "guard 6.4b: more than one $pkg_name package in $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR. Cannot tell which one the ISO carries, so cannot verify either."
    pkg_file=$candidate
    pkg_version=$PKG_VER
    PROVIDED_BINS+=("${PKG_BINS[@]}")
  done

  [[ -n $pkg_file ]] ||
    reject_iso "guard 6.4b: no $pkg_name package in $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR. --local-source should have built one from $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT; its absence means the local build did not run and the ISO's runtime came from the '$BUILD_MIRROR' channel instead -- an unpinned runtime, which is the whole thing this slice removes."

  case $pkg_version in
    *".g$RUNTIME_SHORT"*) ;;
    *) reject_iso "guard 6.4b: $pkg_name is version '$pkg_version', which does not carry iso/RUNTIME's commit ($RUNTIME_SHORT). Both PKGBUILDs derive pkgver from the source tree's git history, so this package was NOT built from $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD. The runtime pin did not take effect." ;;
  esac
  build_log "guard 6.4b: $pkg_name $pkg_version built from $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_SHORT (${#PKG_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries)"
done

(( ${#PROVIDED_BINS[@]} > 0 )) ||
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b: the built packages ship no usr/bin members at all. That is not a runtime; refusing to report a pass."

missing_bins=()
for want in "${REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]}"; do
  found=0
  for have in "${PROVIDED_BINS[@]}"; do
    [[ $want == "$have" ]] && { found=1; break; }
  done
  (( found )) || missing_bins+=("$want")
done

if (( ${#missing_bins[@]} > 0 )); then
  reject_iso "guard 6.4b: the ISO's orchestrator calls binaries the packages it carries do not contain.

  installer   $UPSTREAM_SLUG@$upstream_head (iso/UPSTREAM)
  runtime     $RUNTIME_SLUG@$RUNTIME_HEAD (iso/RUNTIME)

  Missing from $RUNTIME_PACKAGE + $SETTINGS_PACKAGE (${#PROVIDED_BINS[@]} usr/bin entries between them):
$(printf '    /usr/bin/%s\n' "${missing_bins[@]}")
  Guard 6.4a passed on the checkout, so the file exists in bin/ and the
  PKGBUILD did not install it -- check pkgbuilds/$RUNTIME_PACKAGE's package()
  exclusion list in the pkgs checkout."
fi

build_log "guard 6.4b OK: all ${#REQUIRED_TARGET_BINS[@]} orchestrator-called binaries are present in the packages this ISO carries"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Guard 6.5b -- the payload audit, re-asked of what the build actually produced.
#
# 6.5a read TREES. This reads the offline mirror, which is packages, and that
# is the half that matters most: a sudoers drop-in shipped INSIDE a
# .pkg.tar.zst lands on the installed system exactly like one baked into
# airootfs, and no tree scan would ever see it. It is also the route our own
# future omarchy-deck package would take (T5-fork-plan.md §5.4 names that
# package as one of the four roots), so this is the one place the audit can
# catch us shipping the dev Deck's grant by way of our own packaging.
#
# The mirror is readable from the host because build-iso.sh writes it into a
# bind mount (/var/cache/airootfs/var/cache/omarchy) -- the same reason 6.4b
# can read the built packages at all.
#
# ⚠️ It is the slowest check in this script: the audit opens every
# *.pkg.tar.* in the mirror with bsdtar, which on a full build is ~1250
# archives. That is why it runs once, here, rather than per package -- and why
# 6.5a is the cheap half that runs before Docker.
#
# On any failure this goes through reject_iso, not build_fail: the ISO already
# exists on disk by this point, and an image that would ship blanket
# passwordless root must not keep a name that can be flashed or picked up by a
# later run's *.iso glob.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

if ! run_payload_audit "offline mirror" "$OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR"; then
  reject_iso "guard 6.5b: $(payload_audit_verdict "$PAYLOAD_AUDIT_STATUS")

  root audited
    $OFFLINE_MIRROR_DIR   (the packages this ISO carries)

  A drop-in inside a package installs exactly like one baked into airootfs, so
  this is not a lesser finding than 6.5a's -- it is the likelier one, and the
  route our own omarchy-deck package would take (T5-fork-plan.md §5.4,
  docs/PROGRESS.md §5.17). The ISO has been renamed .rejected above."
fi

build_log "guard 6.5b OK: no blanket passwordless root in any package in the offline mirror"

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------
# 9. Name and report the result. Matches omarchy-iso-make's own
#    <name>-<ref>.iso convention so the artifact is unambiguous about which
#    ref/mirror pair produced it.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------

case $latest_iso in
  *-"$BUILD_ISO_REF".iso) final_iso=$latest_iso ;;
  *) final_iso="${latest_iso%.*}-$BUILD_ISO_REF.iso"; mv -f "$latest_iso" "$final_iso" ;;
esac

build_log "build complete: $final_iso"
build_log "$(du -h "$final_iso" | cut -f1) -- $(sha256sum "$final_iso")"
